Onboard360AI — KB Reorganization & Governance Design
 
Onboard360AI
Knowledge Base Reorganization & Governance Design
Migration to a governed, single-source onboarding KB on iCanvas + Confluence

Status: Draft for review
Owner: Chanakya
Platform: iCanvas agentic platform (Claude architecture + skills)

Contents

1. Purpose & Scope
Onboarding content for enterprise products is currently scattered across multiple Confluence spaces, ServiceNow knowledge articles, databases and spreadsheets, owned by different internal teams. The content is distributed, frequently duplicated, and in many cases outdated. This document defines the target design for consolidating that content into a single governed knowledge base (KB) that the iCanvas agentic platform uses to power multiple chatbots.
The chosen strategy is migration into one canonical KB, fronted by a gated intake pipeline so that new and updated content is reviewed and approved before it can ever reach a chatbot. Skills are deliberately designed as micro, single-responsibility, reusable units rather than one monolithic skill; agents compose them.
2. Problem Statement
• Onboarding content lives across multiple Confluence spaces owned by different teams, plus ServiceNow knowledge articles, databases and Excel sheets.
• Content is duplicated across systems and often outdated, with no single source of truth.
• There is no consistent governance: anyone can edit, and there is no enforced review/approval before content is consumed.
• The iCanvas platform needs a trustworthy, deduplicated, access-controlled KB to drive skills, agents and multiple chatbots.
3. Architectural Decision
Three consolidation patterns were considered: physical migration into one store, federation (index across systems in place), and a curated canonical layer fed by the messy sources. For onboarding content the decision is full migration into one canonical KB with a gated intake pipeline.
Key insight: migration and ongoing intake are not two systems. The one-time migration is a high-volume run through the same lifecycle pipeline built for new content. Build the lifecycle first; treat migration as a seeding event through it.
4. Content Lifecycle
Every knowledge unit moves through a single state machine. The hard rule that makes the whole system safe: chatbots only ever retrieve units in the Published state. Everything else is invisible to them.
Draft → Submitted → In Review → Approved → Published  →  (Review-due / Quarantined / Archived)
State transitions are owned by iCanvas (workflow metadata). Content lives in Confluence. The two are linked but separated in concern: Confluence owns content and version history; iCanvas owns process, state and actor history.
5. Two-Space Confluence Model
Confluence is a write-locked serving layer; iCanvas is the system of record for the workflow. Two spaces separate workflow stage from read-scope:
Space
Writable by
Readable by
Holds
Staging
iCanvas service account only
Admin / authoring staff (read-only); not bots, not general audience
Draft, Submitted, In Review, Approved
Published
iCanvas service account only
Everyone incl. bots (read-only)
Published only
 
Defence in depth: the bot retrieval path requires BOTH that a page is in the Published space AND that its state property = Published. Even if a draft were ever mis-filed, an unstamped page is never surfaced.
• Write access to both spaces is exclusive to the iCanvas service account — so "edit only through iCanvas" is physically enforced, not merely policy.
• Admins and authoring staff get read-only on Staging for oversight; every actual mutation still flows through iCanvas.
6. Roles, Authorization & Segregation of Duties
6.1 Roles
Four distinct roles. Submitter is universal; the other three are category-scoped (per product/domain).
Role
Who
Scope
Submitter
Everyone in the user group
Universal — no grant required
Reviewer
Few, per category
Category-scoped
Approver
Few, per category
Category-scoped
Publisher
Few, per category
Category-scoped
 
A permission is the pair (role, category) — e.g. “approver for HelixOS onboarding.” Two independent dimensions per user: role grants and category scope.
6.2 Two-person approval & segregation of duties
Approval is two-person. The segregation rule: on a single unit's journey, no individual may occupy more than one of the four gate roles. If a person submitted a unit, they cannot review, approve or publish it; if they reviewed it, they cannot also approve it, and so on.
Enforcement at every gate is two stacked checks, both deterministic (hard code, not agent judgment):
1. Authorization — the actor must hold the required (role, category) grant for this unit's category.
2. Segregation of duties — the actor must not appear in this unit's prior-actor history.
Both must pass or the transition is blocked.
6.3 Thin-category edge case
Where a category has too few eligible people to satisfy SoD, use a documented override role: an admin may waive SoD, but only with a mandatory audit-log entry. Overrides are never silent.
7. Content Flow Rules
7.1 Move-on-publish
When a unit reaches Approved, the publishing skill moves (not copies) the page from Staging to Published, carrying its history. Exactly one canonical page exists per unit; there is no shadow copy to drift.
7.2 Pull-back-to-Staging on update
Editing a live page never happens in the Published space. An update starts a new workflow instance in Staging — a checked-out copy of the published page — and runs the full four-gate pipeline. The live page stays untouched and bot-visible throughout, then is overwritten atomically on re-approval.
8. Migration Approach
Migration is a one-time bulk seeding run through the same lifecycle, executed by the Migration Agent. It is deliberately not an auto-publish.
3. Connect & extract — pull items from each source system (Confluence spaces, ServiceNow KB, databases, Excel) via connectors.
4. Normalize — each legacy item enters as a Submitted draft in Staging, conformed to the knowledge-unit schema, with a back-pointer to its origin.
5. Tag — auto-classify product/category, audience, onboarding stage and content type; low-confidence tags flagged for human confirmation.
6. Cluster for dedup — semantic + metadata clustering groups near-duplicate and conflicting items; clusters are presented to domain owners.
7. Batch review & merge — domain owners (with SoD enforced) decide which item is authoritative, merge or supersede the rest, and resolve conflicts using a per-category precedence rule.
8. Publish — approved units go through move-on-publish; superseded items are archived, not deleted.
9. Hand off to steady state — once seeded, the Freshness/Lifecycle Agent keeps the KB from rotting; all subsequent change flows through normal intake.
Rationale: entering legacy content as drafts (never auto-published) guarantees no unreviewed or duplicated content reaches a chatbot during the cleanse. It is more work up front, but it is a one-time cost and the freshness agent sustains the result.
9. Skills Catalogue (Micro & Reusable)
Skills are single-responsibility and composable. No skill both decides and acts on a gate; authorization (S6) and segregation of duties (S7) are deliberately separate so they can be reused at every gate. Agents (Section 10) orchestrate these skills.
