9.1 Skill specifications
S1 — normalize-to-knowledge-unit
Purpose. Convert a raw contribution (free text, pasted article, imported ServiceNow/Confluence/Excel/DB record) into the canonical knowledge-unit schema. Pure transformation only.
Inputs: Raw content + source metadata (origin system, original URL/ID, captured fields).
Outputs: A draft knowledge unit conforming to the schema, with source pointer preserved. No state assigned beyond Draft.
Core logic:
• Map source fields to canonical fields; split monolithic source pages into atomic units where one page covers multiple topics.
• Preserve a back-pointer to the original source system and ID for traceability.
• Leave unknown fields empty rather than guessing; flag missing required fields for the tagging skill.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never assign tags, ownership, or lifecycle decisions — that is other skills' job.
• Never write to Confluence or change any state.
• Never invent content not present in the source.
Reused by: Intake/Authoring Agent, Migration Agent.
S2 — auto-tag-unit
Purpose. Classify a normalized unit along the metadata dimensions that drive scoping, routing and freshness.
Inputs: A normalized knowledge unit.
Outputs: The same unit enriched with product, audience, onboarding-stage, content-type and candidate-owner/category tags, each with a confidence score.
Core logic:
• Assign category (product/domain) — this single tag drives reviewer/approver/publisher routing downstream.
• Tag audience and onboarding stage to support chatbot scoping later.
• Emit confidence scores; low-confidence tags are surfaced to the contributor or reviewer rather than committed silently.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never finalize ownership without a human confirmation step when confidence is low.
• Never alter the body content; tagging is metadata-only.
Reused by: Intake/Authoring Agent, Migration Agent, Review-Support Agent (re-tag on update).
S3 — detect-duplicates
Purpose. Find existing units that overlap with an incoming one, so duplication is caught at the source instead of cleaned up later.
Inputs: A candidate unit + the published/approved corpus index.
Outputs: A ranked list of overlapping units with similarity scores and the type of overlap (near-identical, partial, contradictory).
Core logic:
• Hybrid match: semantic similarity plus metadata (same product + stage raises weight).
• Classify each hit as duplicate, partial-overlap, or potential-conflict.
• Return candidates only — the merge/supersede decision is always human.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never auto-merge or auto-delete any unit.
• Never block submission; it advises, the workflow decides.
Reused by: Intake/Authoring Agent (pre-submit warning), Migration Agent (batch clustering).
S4 — diff-against-published
Purpose. Produce a human-readable diff between a submitted update and the currently published version it intends to replace.
Inputs: A submitted unit + its published counterpart (if any).
Outputs: A structured diff (added / removed / changed) plus a plain-language change summary.
Core logic:
• Field-level and body-level diff; highlight changes to safety-critical or compliance-relevant sections.
• Flag any claim that contradicts other published units (calls S3 conflict signal).
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never apply the change; it only describes it for the reviewer.
• Never hide a change because it looks minor.
Reused by: Review-Support Agent, Publishing Agent (pre-publish confirmation).
S5 — validate-against-template
Purpose. Check a unit for completeness and structural compliance against the template defined for its content type.
Inputs: A unit + the content-type template ruleset.
Outputs: Pass/fail with a list of missing or malformed required sections.
Core logic:
• Enforce required sections per content type (e.g. prerequisites, steps, owner, last-reviewed).
• Return actionable gaps, not just a score.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never approve or publish — it gates progression but does not authorize it.
• Never rewrite content to pass itself.
Reused by: Intake/Authoring Agent, Review-Support Agent.
S6 — check-authorization
Purpose. Verify that a given actor holds the required (role, category) grant for the transition they are attempting.
Inputs: Actor identity, attempted transition (review/approve/publish), unit category.
Outputs: Allow / deny, with the reason.
Core logic:
• Look up the actor's grants; require a matching role AND matching category scope.
• Submitter requires no grant; reviewer/approver/publisher are category-scoped.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never use agent judgment — deterministic rule check only.
• Never cache a stale grant; always read current entitlements.
Reused by: Every gated transition (Review-Support, Publishing, Migration agents).
S7 — check-segregation-of-duties
Purpose. Enforce that no individual occupies more than one gate role on a single unit's journey.
Inputs: Actor identity, the unit's prior-actor history, attempted transition.
Outputs: Allow / deny, with the colliding prior action if denied.
Core logic:
• Reject if the actor appears anywhere in the unit's prior-actor history (submitter, reviewer, approver, publisher).
• On thin categories, defer to the configured override policy (admin waiver with audit entry) rather than silently passing.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never allow self-approval or self-review.
• Never waive silently — an override must produce an audit record.
Reused by: Every gated transition; paired with S6 at each gate.
S8 — transition-state
Purpose. Move a unit between lifecycle states and record the actor, timestamp and reason. The single source of workflow truth.
Inputs: Unit ID, from-state, to-state, actor, reason; requires S6 + S7 to have passed.
Outputs: Updated workflow metadata and an appended prior-actor history entry.
Core logic:
• Permit only legal transitions per the state machine.
• Refuse the transition unless both S6 (authorization) and S7 (SoD) returned allow.
• Always append to the immutable actor history.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never transition without recording the actor.
• Never write content to Confluence — it owns state, not content.
Reused by: All workflow agents.
S9 — publish-to-confluence
Purpose. Execute the Approved → Published transition by moving the page from the Staging space to the Published space (move-on-publish).
Inputs: An Approved unit, target Published-space location.
Outputs: A single live page in the Published space, stamped state=Published, with history retained.
Core logic:
• Move (not copy) the page so exactly one canonical page exists.
• Stamp the state property = Published and write the review-due date (calls S12).
• Retire any unit this one supersedes (calls S11).
• Acts only via the iCanvas service account — the sole writer to either space.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never leave a duplicate in Staging.
• Never publish a unit not in Approved state.
• Never run under a human user account.
Reused by: Publishing Agent.
S10 — pullback-to-staging
Purpose. Begin an update cycle for an already-published unit by checking a working copy back into the Staging space.
Inputs: A published unit ID, the submitting actor.
Outputs: A new Draft workflow instance in Staging linked to the live published page.
Core logic:
• Copy the published page into Staging as a fresh Draft; the live page stays untouched and bot-visible.
• Link the draft to its published parent so S9 knows what to overwrite on re-approval.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never edit the live Published page directly.
• Never expose the working copy to bots or the general audience.
Reused by: Intake/Authoring Agent (update path).
S11 — archive-superseded-unit
Purpose. Retire a unit that a newly published one replaces, so old duplicates do not rot in place.
Inputs: The superseded unit ID, the superseding unit ID.
Outputs: Superseded unit moved to Archived state and out of the Published space.
Core logic:
• Set state = Archived, remove from bot-retrievable scope, keep for audit.
• Record the supersession link for traceability.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never hard-delete — archive only.
• Never leave an archived unit bot-retrievable.
Reused by: Publishing Agent, Migration Agent (dedup merge).
S12 — stamp-review-date
Purpose. Set or refresh the review-due / expiry date on a unit at publish time.
Inputs: A unit + the freshness policy for its content type.
Outputs: The unit with a review-due date and expiry date set.
Core logic:
• Compute the date from content-type policy (high-traffic onboarding topics get shorter cycles).
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never publish without a review-due date set.
Reused by: S9 publish-to-confluence.
S13 — scan-freshness
Purpose. Continuously identify units approaching or past their review-due date.
Inputs: The published corpus + freshness policy.
Outputs: Lists of review-due and expired units with their owners.
Core logic:
• Run on a schedule; classify into approaching, due, overdue.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never change state itself — it reports; S14/S8 act.
Reused by: Freshness/Lifecycle Agent.
S14 — quarantine-stale-unit
Purpose. Pull an expired, un-re-approved unit out of the Published (bot-visible) scope.
Inputs: An expired unit past its grace window.
Outputs: Unit set to a Review-due/Quarantined state and removed from bot retrieval.
Core logic:
• Move out of Published scope; notify owner (calls S15); preserve for re-approval.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never auto-edit content.
• Never quarantine before the configured grace window.
Reused by: Freshness/Lifecycle Agent.
S15 — notify-actor
Purpose. Send a targeted notification to the right person for the current step.
Inputs: Recipient(s), event type, unit reference.
Outputs: A delivered notification (review requested, approval pending, review-due, quarantined).
Core logic:
• Resolve the correct recipient from role+category; route to the configured channel.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never notify outside the unit's authorized actor set.
Reused by: All workflow agents.
S16 — retrieve-published
Purpose. The only retrieval path chatbots use. Returns published knowledge scoped to the asking bot.
Inputs: A query + the bot's read-scope (categories/audiences it is allowed to surface).
Outputs: Ranked published units within scope.
Core logic:
• Require BOTH conditions: page is in the Published space AND state property = Published (defence in depth).
• Further filter to the bot's allowed categories/audiences — scoped views over one shared KB, never copies.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never return anything not stamped Published, regardless of space.
• Never read from Staging.
Reused by: Chatbot Retrieval Agent (all chatbots).
S17 — record-audit-event
Purpose. Append an immutable audit entry for any consequential action.
Inputs: Actor, action, unit, before/after state, reason, timestamp.
Outputs: An appended audit-log record.
Core logic:
• Capture every gate decision, override, publish, archive and quarantine.
Hard constraints (must not):
• Never mutate or delete existing audit records.
Reused by: Every agent and every gated skill.
10. Agents (Skill Orchestrators)
Agents are thin orchestrators that sequence micro-skills and apply the gates. The deterministic checks (S6, S7) and the audit log (S17) appear across multiple agents — which is exactly why they are micro-skills.
Agent
Composes
Responsibility
Intake / Authoring Agent
S1, S2, S3, S5, S10, S15
Front door for contributors. Normalizes input, tags it, warns on duplicates before submit, validates completeness, and for updates pulls the published page back to Staging. Produces a Submitted draft.
Review-Support Agent
S2, S4, S5, S6, S7, S8, S15, S17
Assists the human reviewer: shows diffs, flags contradictions, checks template completeness, runs authorization + SoD gate checks, and records the In-Review → Approved (or rejected) transition the human decides.
Publishing Agent
S4, S6, S7, S8, S9, S11, S12, S15, S17
Executes Approved → Published via the service account: move-on-publish, stamp review date, archive superseded units. Gated by S6 + S7 (publisher must differ from submitter/reviewer/approver).
Freshness / Lifecycle Agent
S13, S14, S15, S8, S17
Runs continuously. Finds review-due/expired units, notifies owners, quarantines stale content out of bot scope. Stops the KB rotting back to its pre-migration state.
Migration Agent
S1, S2, S3, S5, S11
One-time bulk seeding. Pulls each legacy item in as a Submitted draft, clusters for dedup, routes batches to domain owners for review/merge — never auto-publishes.
Chatbot Retrieval Agent
S16
The runtime read path. Each chatbot is a scoped configuration over the single published KB; this agent enforces Published-only + scope filtering.
11. Chatbots as Scoped Views
Each chatbot is a configuration over the single published KB — not a copy of the data. A chatbot is defined by its read-scope (allowed categories/audiences), persona and the skills it may call. All of them retrieve exclusively through S16 (retrieve-published), which enforces Published-only plus scope filtering. Adding a chatbot is a config change, never a data duplication.
12. Open Decisions
• Per-category precedence rule for conflict resolution during migration (which source wins when two disagree).
• Freshness cadence per content type (review-due interval and grace window before quarantine).
• Override policy details for thin categories (who may waive SoD, and approval path for the waiver).
• Connector inventory and field mappings for each source system (ServiceNow, each Confluence space, named databases, spreadsheets).
• Knowledge-unit schema field list — to be finalized as the immediate next step.
13. Immediate Next Step
Finalize the knowledge-unit schema: the canonical field list, which fields live as Confluence page properties vs iCanvas workflow metadata, and the label set that drives both bot scoping (S16) and the freshness agent (S13/S14). The schema underpins every skill above.
